<section xml:id="sec-Example">
  <title>Example</title>

  <p>
    Welcome to <pretext />.
    Let's type up some math.
    According to Pythagoras, <m>a^2 + b^2 = c^2</m>.
    But he also thought <m>\sqrt{2}</m> was rational, so maybe we shouldn't take his word for it.
  </p>

  <p>
    Let's collect some info about squares to explore whether this is really true.
  </p>

  <table>
    <title>Perfect Squares</title>

    <tabular halign="center">
      <row bottom="minor" header="yes">
        <cell><m>n</m></cell>
        <cell><m>n^2</m></cell>
      </row>

      <row>
        <cell>1</cell>
        <cell>1</cell>
      </row>

      <row>
        <cell>2</cell>
        <cell>4</cell>
      </row>

      <row>
        <cell>3</cell>
        <cell>9</cell>
      </row>

      <row>
        <cell>4</cell>
        <cell>16</cell>
      </row>

      <row>
        <cell>5</cell>
        <cell>25</cell>
      </row>

      <row>
        <cell><m>\vdots</m></cell>
        <cell><m>\vdots</m></cell>
      </row>
    </tabular>
  </table>

  <theorem xml:id="thm-Pythagorean">
    <title>Pythagorean Theorem</title>

    <statement>
      <p>
        Suppose a triangle has side lengths <m>a, b</m> and hypotenuse length <m>c</m>.
        Then
        <md>
          <mrow> a^2 + b^2 = c^2. </mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
    </statement>


    <proof>
      <image source="example.png" width="50%">
        <shortdescription>
          Right triangle with squares appended to each side.
        </shortdescription>

        <description>
          <p>
            Right triangle with legs labeled <m>a</m> and <m>b</m> and hypotenuse labeled <m>c</m>.
            Squares are appended to each side of the triangle.
          </p>
        </description>
      </image>

      <p>
        As we can see in the image...
      </p>
    </proof>
  </theorem>

  <p>
    We can also draw the picture ourselves:
  </p>

  <figure xml:id="triangle">
    <caption>Triangle</caption>
    <image width="50%">
      <shortdescription>
        Triangle
      </shortdescription>

      <description>
        <p>
          Longer description that can include structure like <m>\alpha</m>.
        </p>
      </description>
      <latex-image>
        \begin{tikzpicture}
            \draw (0,0) to (0, 2) to (2, 0) to (0, 0);
        \end{tikzpicture}
      </latex-image>
    </image>
  </figure>

  <definition xml:id="def-rational">
    <statement>
      <p>
        A real number <m>x \in \R</m> is called <term>rational</term> if there are integers <m>a, b</m> so that <m>x = \frac{a}{b}</m>.
        Otherwise, <m>x</m> is called <term>irrational</term>, no matter what Pythagoras says.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <example>
    <p>
      <m>\frac{1}{2}</m> is rational.
    </p>
  </example>

  <example>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Is <m>\sqrt{2}</m> rational?
      </p>
    </statement>

    <hint>
      <p>
        Why does <m>\pi = \frac{\sqrt{2}}{1}</m> <em>not</em> show that <m>\sqrt{2}</m> is rational?
      </p>
    </hint>

    <hint>
      <p>
        What would happen if you assumed <m>\sqrt{2}</m> is rational?
      </p>
    </hint>

    <answer>
      <p>
        No!
      </p>
    </answer>

    <solution>
      <p>
        Let <m>\sqrt{2} = \frac{a}{b}</m> for relatively prime integers <m>a, b</m>.
        Then:
        <md>
          <mrow>2 \amp = \frac{a^2}{b^2}</mrow>
          <mrow>2b^2 \amp = a^2</mrow>
        </md>
        But, after taking the prime factorization of both sides, the left side has an odd number of prime factors and the right side has an even number.
        This is a contradiction, so <m>\sqrt{2}</m> is irrational.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Prove that <m>\sqrt{p}</m> is irrational for any prime <m>p</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <p>
    Before trying the following exercises, review <xref ref="thm-Pythagorean"/>.
  </p>

